views.py
from django.http import HttpResponse
def learn_django(request):
    return HttpResponse('Hello Django')
The first parameter is always the HttpRequest object.

from django.shortcuts import render
def learn_django(request):
    return render(request, 'courseone.html')
render() combines a template with data and returns an HttpResponse.

def learn_django(request):
    data = {'nm': 'Django', 'du': '4 Months', 'st': 10}
    return render(request, 'courseone.html', context=data)

A filter changes a variable before showing it. Use the pipe symbol:
{{ name|upper }}
{{ value|capfirst }}
{{ value|default:'N/A' }}
{{ some_list|slice:':2' }}
{{ name|length }}

{% if nm %}
    <h1>Hello {{nm}}</h1>
{% endif %}
Conditions work too: {% if nm == 'Django' and st == 5 %} ... {% endif %}
Filters work: {% if nm|length >= 6 %}

{% if nm %}
    Hello {{nm}}
{% elif st %}
    Seats {{st}}
{% else %}
    No Course Available
{% endif %}
Always close with {% endif %}.

{% load static %}
<link rel='stylesheet' href="{% static 'css/style.css' %}">
{% load static %} must come first, then {% static 'path' %} gives the file's URL.

Put common code (header, footer, links) in base.html and create blocks for parts that change:
<title>{% block title %}Other{% endblock %}</title>
{% block content %}{% endblock %}

home.html
{% extends 'base.html' %}
{% block title %}Home{% endblock %}
{% block content %}<h1>Hello Home</h1>{% endblock %}
The child only fills the blocks.

Insert one template inside another:
{% include 'topcourse.html' %}
Pass extra data: {% include 'topcourse.html' with p='PHP' d='Django' %}
Add 'only' to pass just those variables.

